Trebor A. Rude wrote:
> I've been trying to play Healers recently, and not having much success. I'm
> used to playing combat wombats, and that just doesn't work for Healers.
> Does anyone have any advice on what to do in the early game? I usually try
> to upgrade my scalpel to a elven dagger, then to a unicorn horn when I have
> the chance. I tend to forget about the e-word (though I have no problem
> using it if I remember). How effective is writing it in the dust (because
> in the early game, that's usually the only choice)? What about armor? I've
> generally been avoiding metallic armor (except little stuff like orcish
> helms, which don't seem to impact failure rates too much). Should I try the
> protection racket (can't imagine how I'd survive a dark level of the Mines,
> though)? Any advice would be appreciated.

One way to play Healers is to have pets. Lots of pets. Since you have a 
stethoscope and flesh to stone, keeping your pets healthy and fed isn't 
such a problem. It's quite tedious to get 3 large dogs and 3 large cats 
from level to level without a whistle (I'm speaking from experience :), 
but the combat effectivness of such a large pack is quite incredible :).

Also, the protection racket might be a good thing to try, since that 
will teach you a lot of tactics and tricks that the average combat 
wombat doesn't need (such as, relying on your pets, using anything you 
have to gain even a small advantage, etc, as opposed to just smashing 
monsters into small piles of putrid debris). Of course, trying the 
protection racket means you'll lose quite a few characters who might 
have made it further without the racket, only to give one character an 
advanage, which might or might not bother you, depending on your play 
style... And if you try the protection racket, I'd suggest choosing a 
gnomish Healer, makes the mines easier.

And about armor, in the begining, I'd say to use every bit of armor you 
can find and rely on pets, wands and scrolls in the brginning. If you 
need to heal up or flesh to stone some stuff in between battles, then 
surround yourself with your pets and take of your armor to cast the 
spells then dress up again...

Well, hth, happy hacking.
